Audio driver for the Google Pixel 7

All audio of the phone hangs behind the AoC ("always-on compute") DSP. The
driver 'pixel_audio' starts the AoC through the security chip (GSA) and
Trusty, registers the AoC's sound card with the two CS35L41 speaker
amplifiers on SPI7 as its codecs, and connects the card to Genode's audio
sessions. It is a port of the vendor's Linux drivers: the Trusty client
(SMC core, virtio transport, TIPC), the GSA client, the AoC core with its
mailboxes, ALSA and ASoC with the AoC's machine driver 'aoc_alsa' (without
compress offload, in-call, VoIP, USB, and DisplayPort), and Google's forks
of the CS35L41 codec driver and of wm_adsp on the vendor's SPI driver. It
needs the ports 'pixel_linux', 'google_soc_gs', 'google_trusty',
'google_aoc', and 'google_amplifiers'.

The driver plays the Record sessions "left" and "right" on the playback
PCM named by its config, feeds the Play sessions "mic_left" and
"mic_right" from the capture PCM, applies the mixer settings of the
config, and reports all mixer controls ("mixer").

Trusty is entered with SMCs through core's system-control interface, so
the driver must be started with 'managing_system: yes'. All SMCs are
issued by one entrypoint, the component must keep its CPU (no affinity
change): a standard call to Trusty and its restart have to come from the
same CPU. Trusty asks for CPU time by a doorbell interrupt (SGI 13), which
cannot reach a component; a thread of the driver enters Trusty
periodically instead. The GSA reads the AoC firmware through its stage-2
protection unit (S2MPU), which the boot loader leaves without access; the
driver opens it for all memory, as pKVM does on Android, around the
authentication of the firmware.


Bring-up
~~~~~~~~

The devices are probed one after the other. The bring-up always runs
completely, up to the first failure. Each stage logs one result line:

! pixel_audio: stage <name> ok: ...
! pixel_audio: stage <name> FAILED: ... <raw return values>

:smc:   Fast calls to Trusty: version string, API version.
:gsa:   Probe of the GSA mailbox, one read-only command (SJTAG status).
:tipc:  Probe of the Trusty core and its virtio/TIPC transport, the AoC
        state from the GSA hardware manager.
:load:  Probe of the AoC mailboxes and the AoC core, firmware load
        ('aoc.bin'), authentication by the GSA.
:start: The AoC is started; the stage waits for it to come online.
:card:  Probe of SPI7 and the two amplifiers (logged as stage 'amp'), then
        the sound card.
:mixer: All controls of the card are listed and read once, the result is
        the report "mixer".
:play:  The Record sessions are opened and the card task is started, which
        applies the config and feeds the playback PCM. The stage does not
        wait for it.

Each applied config logs its own line (see "Configuration"). When the playback
PCM has consumed four periods for the first time, the driver logs
"pixel_audio: playback: PCM <n> running: ...".

A step that does not advance for 5 seconds is reported ("pixel_audio:
still in step ..."): several waits of the vendor code have no timeout.


Settings
~~~~~~~~

The ROM 'settings' holds what is fixed for a boot:

! settings | aoc_log: no
! + firmware aoc.bin | size: 21328448

:online_timeout_ms: how long stage 'start' waits for the AoC (default 10000).
:nop_poll_ms:       period of the entries into Trusty (default 200).
:verbose:           log every control write (default 'no').
:aoc_log:           forward the log of the AoC firmware (default 'no'):
                    each line of its service "logging" is printed as
                    "pixel_audio: AOC: <line>", at most 'aoc_log_rate' lines
                    per second after a burst of 'aoc_log_burst' (defaults 20
                    and 300). 'aoc_log_replies' prints the replies of the
                    commands that succeeded too. A line blocks the driver
                    for the time it takes on the serial line (about 10 ms).

A 'firmware' node gives the exact size of a firmware image, a ROM
dataspace is page-rounded.


Configuration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The ROM 'config' is the mixer policy, it can change at run time:

! config | route: media
! + default Out Format | value: S16_LE
! + control Audio DSP Mode | value: Ambient
! + control PCM Source | value: ASP
! + playback | pcm: 1 | enabled: yes

* 'default' nodes are the initial values of controls, written once with
  the first config, unless a control has the value already.

* 'control' nodes are writes by control name, in order. The value is an
  integer, a space-separated list of integers (one per element), or the
  text of an enum item; 'index' writes one element. A control the previous
  config wrote and the new one does not name is set back to its default.

* A 'stream' node opens and prepares a PCM (48 kHz, stereo, 16 bit), which
  makes the AoC set up the stream, without data. A 'tuning' node is sent
  to the AoC service 'audio_output_tuning'.

* 'playback' and 'capture' name the PCMs of the sessions. The playback PCM
  is opened when the Record sessions deliver samples and closed when they
  are depleted, so that the amplifiers power down in between. The capture
  PCM runs while its node is enabled.

An unknown control or a failing write is logged as a warning and skipped.
Each applied config is summarized in one line ("pixel_audio: config N
applied: ...").


Sessions and ROMs
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Platform and Dma (devices 'gsa', 's2mpu_gsa', 'aoc', 'aoc_mbox0' to
'aoc_mbox2', 'spi7'), Pin_control, Pin_state, and IRQ of gs201_pio (chip
selects 'gpp14-3' and 'gpp22-1', resets 'gpp17-1' and 'gpp25-2',
interrupts '79' and '94'), Timer, Record, Play, Report ('mixer'), and the
ROMs 'dtb' (built with the driver), 'settings', 'config', 'aoc.bin', and
the amplifiers' firmware 'cs35l41-dsp1-spk-prot.wmfw',
'cs35l41-dsp1-spk-prot.bin', 'R-cs35l41-dsp1-spk-prot.bin'. The firmware is
not part of Genode; without the amplifiers' firmware they play only with
'PCM Source' = ASP. Sessions are opened when a stage needs them.

The value rules of the config have host tests:

! make -C repos/pixel/src/driver/audio/pixel/test

The tool 'tool/pull_audio_firmware' copies the firmware and the vendor's
mixer paths from a phone running Android into the tar archive
'contrib/pixel_audio_firmware.tar'. The scenario 'run/audio_pixel' plays a
sine tone, or the WAV file named by WAV, on the loudspeakers, with a config
for media without speaker protection that 'tool/audio_speaker_config'
generates from the mixer paths:

! repos/pixel/tool/pull_audio_firmware
! make run/audio_pixel KERNEL=hw BOARD=google_pixel7 [WAV=<file>]
